\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:setup}

We use Pythia-410M-deduped \citep{biderman2023pythia} as our base model and fine-tune on a 50K-subset of OpenWebText.
Starting from the pretrained checkpoint (generation~0), each subsequent generation fine-tunes on synthetic text produced by the previous generation, for a total of 10 recursive generations.
At each generation we compute the spectral indicators from \S\ref{subsec:spectral_metrics} on the Gram matrix of 1K held-out last-layer embeddings, paired with three downstream metrics: perplexity, HellaSwag accuracy (0-shot), and distinct-4.

For the mixing comparison (\S\ref{subsec:phase1}), we evaluate three strategies sharing the same total real-data budget:
(1)~\textbf{pure collapse} ($r_t{=}0$),
(2)~\textbf{fixed mix} ($r_t{=}0.50$), and
(3)~\textbf{adaptive} ($k{=}10$, Eq.~\ref{eq:controller}).

\subsection{Spectral Collapse Characterization}
\label{subsec:phase0}

Table~\ref{tab:phase0} reports spectral and downstream metrics across 10 recursive generations, which reveal a three-phase structure.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrrrrc@{}}
\toprule
Gen & $r_{\text{eff}}$ & SLV & PPL & HeSw & D-4 & $\sigma_1$ \\
\midrule
0  & 417.55 & 2366.68 &  9.55 & .413 & .741 & 1942.8 \\
2  & \textbf{496.44} & \textbf{2469.93} & 13.58 & .376 & .515 &  712.0 \\
5  & 479.21 & 2370.17 & 24.59 & .344 & .463 &  741.0 \\
7  & 464.20 & 2304.19 & 38.89 & .319 & .447 &  791.3 \\
10 & 442.46 & 2178.95 & 76.78 & .295 & .426 &  825.7 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Spectral and downstream metrics at selected generations (Pythia-410M; full trajectory described in text). Bold marks the spectral peak before collapse onset.}
\label{tab:phase0}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/spectral_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{SLV across 10 recursive generations of Pythia-410M under pure-synthetic training (solid) and 50\% real-data mixing (dashed). Three phases are visible in the pure-synthetic trajectory: decompression (Gen~0--2, SLV peaks at 2470), onset (Gen~2--4), and acceleration (Gen~4--10, SLV falls 12\% from peak to 2179). Under 50\% mixing, SLV rises monotonically (except a negligible Gen~1 dip) to 2572 at Gen~10, 8.7\% above baseline and 102 points above the pure-collapse peak.}
\label{fig:spectral_trajectory}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Phase I: Spectral Decompression (Gen~0--2).}
We identify phase boundaries at inflection points where $r_{\text{eff}}$ and SLV transition from rising to declining.
In the first two generations, $r_{\text{eff}}$ rises from 417.55 to 496.44 (+18.9\%) and SLV increases from 2366.68 to 2469.93 (+4.4\%), while $\sigma_1$ drops sharply from 1942.8 to 712.0 as spectral mass redistributes from the dominant direction to smaller components.
Crucially, this decompression does not indicate improved model quality: HellaSwag drops from 0.413 to 0.376 ($-$8.9\%) and distinct-4 falls from 0.741 to 0.515 ($-$30.5\%) during the same interval.
HellaSwag already declines 5.6\% from Gen~0 to Gen~1, precisely when spectral indicators are still rising; this asymmetry suggests that decompression captures distributional broadening rather than preservation of task-supporting representations.
A monitoring system relying solely on spectral metrics during Phase~I would incorrectly conclude the model remains healthy; joint spectral-downstream tracking~\citep{gu2026sigma} is essential to detect early collapse.
The value of SLV lies not in temporal precedence over downstream metrics, but in requiring no text generation or external benchmark evaluation; it supports continuous monitoring at training time.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:setup}

We use Pythia-410M-deduped \citep{biderman2023pythia} as our base model and fine-tune on a 50K-subset of OpenWebText.
Starting from the pretrained checkpoint (generation~0), each subsequent generation fine-tunes on synthetic text produced by the previous generation, for a total of 10 recursive generations.
At each generation we compute the spectral indicators from \S\ref{subsec:spectral_metrics} on the Gram matrix of 1K held-out last-layer embeddings, paired with three downstream metrics: perplexity, HellaSwag accuracy (0-shot), and distinct-4.

For the mixing comparison (\S\ref{subsec:phase1}), we evaluate three strategies sharing the same total real-data budget:
(1)~\textbf{pure collapse} ($r_t{=}0$),
(2)~\textbf{fixed mix} ($r_t{=}0.50$), and
(3)~\textbf{adaptive} ($k{=}10$, Eq.~\ref{eq:controller}).

\subsection{Spectral Collapse Characterization}
\label{subsec:phase0}

Table~\ref{tab:phase0} reports spectral and downstream metrics across 10 recursive generations, which reveal a three-phase structure.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrrrrc@{}}
\toprule
Gen & $r_{\text{eff}}$ & SLV & PPL & HeSw & D-4 & $\sigma_1$ \\
\midrule
0  & 417.55 & 2366.68 &  9.55 & .413 & .741 & 1942.8 \\
2  & \textbf{496.34} & \textbf{2468.28} & 13.57 & .378 & .510 &  715.1 \\
5  & 479.64 & 2367.65 & 24.60 & .341 & .440 &  747.3 \\
7  & 464.19 & 2297.87 & 38.17 & .319 & .419 &  790.7 \\
10 & 440.62 & 2194.86 & 73.64 & .293 & .410 &  855.9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Spectral and downstream metrics at selected generations (Pythia-410M, mean of seeds 42 and 43; sample s.d.\ at Gen~10: $r_{\text{eff}}$ $\pm$2.6, SLV $\pm$22.5, PPL $\pm$4.4, HeSw $\pm$.004). Bold marks the spectral peak.}
\label{tab:phase0}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/spectral_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{SLV across 10 recursive generations of Pythia-410M under pure-synthetic training (solid) and 50\% real-data mixing (dashed). Three phases are visible in the pure-synthetic trajectory: decompression (Gen~0--2, SLV peaks at 2468), onset (Gen~2--4), and acceleration (Gen~4--10, SLV falls 11\% from peak to 2195). Under 50\% mixing, SLV rises monotonically (except a negligible Gen~1 dip) to 2572 at Gen~10, 8.7\% above baseline and 104 points above the pure-collapse peak.}
\label{fig:spectral_trajectory}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Phase I: Spectral Decompression (Gen~0--2).}
We identify phase boundaries at inflection points where $r_{\text{eff}}$ and SLV transition from rising to declining.
In the first two generations, $r_{\text{eff}}$ rises from 417.6 to 496.3 (+18.9\%) and SLV increases from 2366.7 to 2468.3 (+4.3\%), while $\sigma_1$ drops sharply from 1942.8 to 715.1 as spectral mass redistributes from the dominant direction to smaller components.
Crucially, this decompression does not indicate improved model quality: HellaSwag drops from 0.413 to 0.378 ($-$8.5\%) and distinct-4 falls from 0.741 to 0.510 ($-$31.2\%) during the same interval.
HellaSwag already declines 5.6\% from Gen~0 to Gen~1, precisely when spectral indicators are still rising; this asymmetry suggests that decompression captures distributional broadening rather than preservation of task-supporting representations.
A monitoring system relying solely on spectral metrics during Phase~I would incorrectly conclude the model remains healthy; joint spectral-downstream tracking~\citep{gu2026sigma} is essential to detect early collapse.
The value of SLV lies not in temporal precedence over downstream metrics, but in requiring no text generation or external benchmark evaluation; it supports continuous monitoring at training time.

\paragraph{Phase II: Collapse Onset (Gen~2--4).}
Both $r_{\text{eff}}$ and SLV reverse after Gen~2 ($r_{\text{eff}}$: 496.3$\to$493.7, SLV: 2468$\to$2439), and this reversal marks the transition from decompression to collapse while perplexity continues its monotonic rise.

\paragraph{Phase III: Accelerating Collapse (Gen~4--10).}
Spectral contraction accelerates: the mean per-generation $r_{\text{eff}}$ decline in Phase~III is $1.67 \pm 0.18$\%/gen (mean $\pm$ s.d.\ across two seeds).
By Gen~10, $r_{\text{eff}}$ falls to 440.6 ($-$11.2\% from peak), SLV drops to 2194.9 ($-$7.3\% from baseline), perplexity reaches 73.6 ($+$671\%), and $\sigma_1$ reverses its earlier decline, reaching 855.9 at Gen~10---a sign of renewed spectral concentration (Figure~\ref{fig:spectral_trajectory}).

\subsection{Cross-Scale Validation}
\label{subsec:cross_scale}

Repeating the protocol on Pythia-1B (Gen~0--3) confirms the same decompression-onset structure ($r_{\text{eff}}$ peak at Gen~2, SLV peak one generation earlier). SLV's low cost and monotonic post-peak decline make it well-suited as an automated collapse monitor.

\subsection{Adaptive Mixing Comparison}
\label{subsec:phase1}

Under pure synthetic training (\S\ref{subsec:phase0}), SLV peaks at Gen~2 (2468) and declines monotonically thereafter.
With 50\% real-data mixing, SLV rises over nine of ten generations, reaching 2572 at Gen~10 (104 points above the pure-collapse peak); a negligible dip at Gen~1 ($\Delta_1 \approx 0.0003$, well below $\tau$) reflects the initial fine-tuning transient.

The adaptive controller ($k{=}10$) remains silent through all ten generations: $\Delta_t$ is non-positive from Gen~2 onward, and the sole positive value ($\Delta_1 \approx 0.0003$) falls $6\times$ below $\tau{=}0.002$.
Since $\Delta_t \leq 0$ for all $t \geq 2$, the controller stays silent for any finite $k$ and any $\tau > 0$ (\S\ref{subsec:controller}); larger gain values do not escape the paradox.
Since pure collapse ($r{=}0$) produces SLV decline from Gen~3 while fixed mix ($r{=}0.5$) yields sustained growth from Gen~2, and $r{=}0.2$ falls below $r_{\text{crit}}$ (\S\ref{subsec:phase_diagram_exp}), we bound $0.2 < r_{\text{crit}} \leq 0.5$.

Despite sustained spectral expansion, downstream metrics degrade steadily: perplexity increases 58\% ($9.55 \to 15.10$) and distinct-4 falls 87\% ($0.741 \to 0.095$) over ten generations---the lowest D-4 of any condition, below even pure collapse ($r{=}0$: 0.410).
This reinforces the asymmetry from \S\ref{subsec:phase0}: spectral health under mixed training does not guarantee model health, and the signal attenuation paradox renders per-generation reactive controllers ineffective precisely when collapse prevention succeeds.

\subsection{Alternative EWS Comparison}
\label{subsec:alt_ews}

To assess whether the spectral patterns in \S\ref{subsec:phase0} reflect a broader embedding-level geometric phenomenon rather than an artifact of a single metric, we compare SLV against six alternative collapse indicators from three signal sources: embedding geometry (RankMe, isotropy), attention weights (stable rank, spectral norm), and output distributions (token entropy, perplexity growth rate).

\subsection{Spectral Metrics}
\label{subsec:spectral_metrics}

Given a language model at generation $t$, we extract last-layer embeddings for $n{=}1{,}000$ held-out samples to form $\mathbf{E} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d}$ and compute the Gram matrix $\mathbf{G} = \mathbf{E}^\top \mathbf{E}$.
Let $\sigma_1 \geq \sigma_2 \geq \cdots \geq \sigma_d$ denote the singular values of $\mathbf{E}$.
We track three complementary spectral indicators:

\paragraph{Spectral Log-Volume (SLV).}
$\text{SLV} = \sum_i \log \sigma_i = \frac{1}{2} \log \det \mathbf{G}$.
This quantity measures the total volume spanned by the embedding cloud in log-space.
Because it sums over all singular values, SLV is sensitive to contraction along any spectral direction, not only the dominant ones.

\paragraph{Effective Rank.}
$r_{\text{eff}} = \exp(H(\mathbf{p}))$, where $p_i = \sigma_i / \sum_j \sigma_j$ and $H$ is the Shannon entropy, measuring how many dimensions carry substantial variance (uniform $\to d$, concentrated $\to 1$).

\paragraph{Leading Singular Value.}
$\sigma_1$ tracks the dominant spectral direction; rising $\sigma_1$ under falling $r_{\text{eff}}$ signals representational collapse into a low-dimensional subspace.

\subsection{Adaptive Mixing Controller}
\label{subsec:controller}

We propose a closed-loop controller that adjusts the real-data mixing ratio $r_t$ at each generation based on the rate of spectral change.
The update rule is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:controller}
r_{t+1} = \frac{B_{\text{rem}}}{T - t} + k \cdot \max(0,\; \Delta_t - \tau)
\end{equation}
where $B_{\text{rem}}$ is the remaining real-data budget (fraction of total budget not yet consumed), $T$ is the total number of generations, $\tau{=}0.002$ is a sensitivity threshold, and $k{=}10$ is the controller gain.
The signal $\Delta_t$ is the baseline-normalized SLV drop: $\Delta_t = (\widetilde{S}_{t-1} - \widetilde{S}_t) / |\widetilde{S}_0|$, where $\widetilde{S}_t$ is the exponential moving average (EMA)-smoothed SLV ($\alpha{=}0.5$) and $\widetilde{S}_0$ is initialized from the pretrained model (Gen~0, SLV${=}2366.68$).
The signed form ensures that spectral decompression ($\Delta_t < 0$) does not trigger the controller; only contraction activates the $\max(0, \cdot)$ gate in Eq.~\ref{eq:controller}.
The output is clipped to $[r_{\min}, r_{\max}] = [0.20, 0.80]$ and subject to the global constraint $\sum_t r_t \leq B_{\text{total}}$.

The first term distributes the remaining budget uniformly across future generations; the model degrades gracefully even without spectral signal.
The second term injects additional real data when SLV contraction exceeds the threshold $\tau$, proportional to the severity of the spectral drop.
This design requires no lookahead and operates with a single hyperparameter choice ($k$) beyond the budget constraint.

\paragraph{Controller Silence Condition.}
Define $r_{\text{crit}}$ as the minimum constant mixing ratio such that SLV is non-decreasing from the second generation onward: $\widetilde{S}_t \geq \widetilde{S}_{t-1}$ for all $t \in \{2, \ldots, T\}$ (excluding the initial fine-tuning transient at $t{=}1$).
When a constant ratio $r \geq r_{\text{crit}}$ is applied, $\Delta_t \leq 0 < \tau$ for all $t \geq 2$ and any $\tau > 0$, so the controller boost is identically zero.
This $\tau$-independence means the paradox cannot be resolved by lowering the sensitivity threshold; the same argument extends to cumulative variants.
Our experiments (\S\ref{subsec:phase1}) empirically bound $0.25 < r_{\text{crit}} \leq 0.3$ over Gen~2--10 for Pythia-410M on OpenWebText.

\subsection{Second-Order Spectral Indicator}
\label{subsec:second_order}

The signal attenuation paradox (\S\ref{subsec:controller}) arises because the first-order signal $\Delta_t$ becomes non-positive under sufficient real-data mixing.
We define a second-order indicator based on the rate of change of the SLV growth:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:second_order}
\Delta^2_t = \widetilde{\Delta}_{t-1} - \widetilde{\Delta}_t
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\Delta}_t$ is the EMA-smoothed first-order signal with smoothing parameter $\alpha_2$ (a tunable hyperparameter; we use $\alpha_2{=}0.3$ in our experiments).
Even when SLV rises monotonically ($\Delta_t < 0$ for all $t \geq 2$), a sustained negative $\Delta^2_t$ suggests that the growth rate is generally decelerating, a potential precursor to eventual stagnation or reversal.
Whether this deceleration signal generalizes across mixing ratios remains an open question (\S\ref{sec:conclusion}).

\subsection{Collapse Phase Diagram}
\label{subsec:phase_diagram}

We define the \emph{collapse phase diagram} as the mapping from constant mixing ratio $r$ to the SLV trajectory over $T$ generations.
For each $r$, we classify the outcome: \emph{collapse} if SLV declines monotonically after the decompression phase (Gen~2 onward), \emph{stable} if SLV is non-decreasing, and \emph{transitional} if SLV shows non-monotonic behavior after Gen~2 (both increases and decreases within the observation window).
The critical mixing ratio $r_{\text{crit}}$ (\S\ref{subsec:controller}) is the boundary between collapse and stability in this diagram.
We estimate $r_{\text{crit}}$ by evaluating multiple constant ratios and identifying the lowest $r$ for which $\widetilde{S}_t \geq \widetilde{S}_{t-1}$ holds for all $t \in \{2, \ldots, T\}$.

